<h2>3.5 Detailed Database Schema</h2>
<p>The database is the core of the Zenda system. Below are the primary tables and their relationships:</p>
<h3>3.5.1 Users Table</h3>
<div class='list-item'>• <b>id:</b> UUID (Primary Key)</div>
<div class='list-item'>• <b>name:</b> TEXT (Full name of the user)</div>
<div class='list-item'>• <b>email:</b> TEXT (Unique email for login)</div>
<div class='list-item'>• <b>role:</b> TEXT (student, vendor, or admin)</div>
<div class='list-item'>• <b>kyc_status:</b> TEXT (pending, verified, or rejected)</div>
<div class='list-item'>• <b>escrow_balance:</b> DECIMAL (Current funds held for vendor)</div>

<h3>3.5.2 Installments Table</h3>
<div class='list-item'>• <b>id:</b> UUID (Primary Key)</div>
<div class='list-item'>• <b>order_id:</b> UUID (Foreign Key to Orders)</div>
<div class='list-item'>• <b>total_installments:</b> INTEGER (Number of payments)</div>
<div class='list-item'>• <b>frequency:</b> TEXT (daily, weekly, or monthly)</div>
<div class='list-item'>• <b>remaining_balance:</b> DECIMAL (Amount yet to be paid)</div>
<div class='list-item'>• <b>next_payment_date:</b> DATE (Deadline for next installment)</div>

<br clear='all' style='page-break-before:always'>

<h1>CHAPTER 4: SYSTEM IMPLEMENTATION</h1>
<h2>4.1 Choice of Programming Language and Tools</h2>
<p>The system was implemented using the Javascript ecosystem. React.js was chosen for the frontend due to its component based architecture and efficient rendering. Node.js with Express.js was used for the backend to ensure a unified language across the stack and high performance for concurrent requests. Supabase was selected as the database and authentication provider because of its seamless integration with React and robust security features.</p>

<h2>4.2 Algorithm for Installment Calculation</h2>
<p>The system uses a linear division algorithm to calculate installments. Given a total price (P), a number of installments (N), and a frequency (F):</p>
<div class='list-item'>1. The base installment amount (A) is calculated as P divided by N.</div>
<div class='list-item'>2. A small convenience fee (C) of 2% is added to each installment to cover service costs.</div>
<div class='list-item'>3. The first payment date is set to the current date (down payment).</div>
<div class='list-item'>4. Subsequent dates are calculated by adding the interval of F to the previous date.</div>
<div class='list-item'>5. The system stores these as a plan and triggers the first transaction.</div>

<h2>4.3 Backend Service Logic</h2>
<h3>4.3.1 Vendor Settlement Algorithm</h3>
<p>The vendor settlement process is automated to ensure fairness. When a customer makes a payment, the backend service logs the transaction and increments the vendor's escrow balance. However, the funds remain in a "locked" state. Every 24 hours, a settlement service scans for orders marked as "delivered" where the customer has provided the receipt OTP. Upon finding such orders, the logic transfers the corresponding escrow amount to the vendor's withdrawable balance.</p>

<h2>4.4 System Testing and Validation</h2>
<p>The system underwent rigorous testing including:</p>
<div class='list-item'>• <b>Unit Testing:</b> Testing individual functions like the installment calculator.</div>
<div class='list-item'>• <b>Integration Testing:</b> Verifying that the frontend and backend communicate correctly.</div>
<div class='list-item'>• <b>User Acceptance Testing (UAT):</b> Simulated sessions with students to evaluate the ease of use.</div>

<br clear='all' style='page-break-before:always'>

<h1>CHAPTER 5: SUMMARY, CONCLUSION, AND RECOMMENDATIONS</h1>
<h2>5.1 Summary of Findings</h2>
<p>The implementation of Zenda successfully addressed the core challenges of gadget affordability. The research found that automation significantly reduces the risk of default by maintaining constant communication with the user through reminders. Furthermore, the escrow system was found to be a critical trust builder, encouraging vendors to participate in the installment model.</p>

<h2>5.2 Conclusion</h2>
<p>This project has demonstrated that a well designed fintech ecosystem can effectively lower the barriers to technology acquisition. Zenda provides a secure, transparent, and user friendly platform that meets the needs of both students and vendors. The use of modern cloud technologies has ensured that the system is both scalable and maintainable, making it a viable solution for the Nigerian tech market.</p>

<h2>5.3 Recommendations</h2>
<p>It is recommended that future versions of Zenda incorporate more advanced credit scoring algorithms using machine learning to analyze user behavior. Additionally, direct integration with academic records through university portals could further verify student status and reduce risk. Expanding the system to include other essential academic materials like books and stationery would also be a valuable future enhancement.</p>

